<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</h1><p class="st-meta">1269 CE – 1807 CE · capital Wolfenbüttel</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Guelph principality ruled from Wolfenbüttel, home of the great Herzog August Library and the University of Helmstedt, and forerunner of the Duchy of Brunswick.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the division of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-brunswick-luneburg">Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg</a> in 1269, Albert I the Tall received the southern lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. After further partitions the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> line took shape in the 15th century, with its residence at the castle of Wolfenbüttel, as the city of Brunswick largely governed itself.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">Henry the Younger</a> was a fierce Catholic opponent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, but his son Julius introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in 1568 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-helmstedt">University of Helmstedt</a> in 1576. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-the-younger-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">Augustus the Younger</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzog-august-library">Herzog August Library</a>, one of the great libraries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Lessing</a> later served as librarian. The dukes subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> in 1671 and moved their court there in 1753.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The principality was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a> in 1807. Restored in 1813, it became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-brunswick">Duchy of Brunswick</a> in 1815, which lasted until 1918. The territory is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-wolfenbuttel.jpg" alt="Principality of Wolfenbüttel" width="320" height="300"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzogtum_Braunschweig_1789.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1269 CE – 1807 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wolfenbüttel</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry the Younger, Julius of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel, Augustus the Younger</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel, Braunschweig-Wolfenbüttel</dd></dl></aside></div>
